+# PHASE A.5 — IMPLEMENTATION GAP PLAN
+
+**Status:** Planning / audit only. No application code, migrations, tests, CI configuration, or package files have been modified. Nothing committed, pushed, merged, or deployed.
+
+**Baseline branch:** `origin/feature/global-lead-saas` (NOT `main`, NOT `cline/q2jjjkyh`)
+
+**Why this baseline:** `main` (`bcd5806`) is an ancestor of `origin/feature/global-lead-saas` (merge-base = `bcd5806`). The feature branch already contains the multi-tenant foundation (commit `aa1f0e1`) plus the international connector framework. Branching from `main` would discard that foundation and force reimplementation. Branching from `cline/q2jjjkyh` is also incorrect — it lacks the multi-tenant foundation and contains only the uncommitted `isMainModule` edit.
+
+## 0. Cross-cutting constraints (apply to all components)
+
+- Preserve existing international connectors (`server/connectors/{brreg,companiesHouse,csvImport,framework,osm,webSearch}.js`).
+- Preserve existing tenant isolation (tenant-scoped queries already present on feature branch).
+- Do not introduce breaking API changes without documenting them in this plan.
+- Do not use a shared tenant ID (`1`) as a substitute for real tenancy — every credential/secret must be tied to an explicit, non-default tenant.
+- Never store credentials in plaintext.
+- Never expose secrets through API responses, logs, errors, or audit records.
+- Never allow tests to fall back to a production database.
+- Do not weaken existing SSRF protection (`isPrivateIp`/`validateSafeUrl`/`safeFetch`), authentication (`requireAuth`/`requireRole`), or production safeguards (weak-JWT-secret rejection in production).
+## 1. Credential encryption using AES-256-GCM
+
+### Existing implementation and file paths
+- **None on `main` or `origin/feature/global-lead-saas`.** No encryption library, no `createCipher`/`createDecipher`, no AES-GCM code anywhere in `server.js` or related files.
+- `package.json` on feature branch has no encryption dependency (only `bcryptjs`, `jsonwebtoken`, etc.).
+
+### Missing functionality
+- No credential encryption at rest.
+- No encryption/decryption helper module.
+- No initialization vector (IV) handling, no associated-data (AAD) binding to tenant/context.
+
+### Proposed changes
+- Add a small encryption helper module exposing `encryptCredential(plaintext, key, tenantId)` and `decryptCredential(encrypted, key, tenantId)`.
+- Use Node.js `crypto.createCipheriv('aes-256-gcm', key, iv)` / `createDecipheriv`.
+- Bind AAD to `tenant_id` so ciphertext cannot be replayed across tenants.
+- Generate a random 12-byte IV per encryption; store IV + authTag alongside ciphertext.
+- Export helper for unit testing without a database.
+
+### Database migrations
+- New table `tenant_credentials` (section 3) stores `ciphertext`, `iv`, `auth_tag`, `key_version` — never plaintext.
+
+### API changes
+- None at this layer; exposed via credential API (section 3).
+
+### Security considerations
+- Key material must never be logged. Redact `key` from any error/debug output.
+- IV must be random per operation (`crypto.randomBytes(12)`), never reused with the same key.
+- AES-256-GCM provides confidentiality + integrity; tampered ciphertext must fail decryption before any use.
+- Do not log ciphertext either — treat it as sensitive.
+
+### Tests required
+- Unit tests for encrypt/decrypt: round-trip, wrong key fails, tampered ciphertext fails, IV reuse produces different ciphertext, AAD/tenant binding prevents cross-tenant replay.
+- Test that plaintext never appears in logs (capture `logger` output).
+
+### Dependencies
+- Node.js built-in `crypto` only — no new npm dependency.
+
+### Implementation order
+- Implement before section 3.
+
+### Acceptance criteria
+- encrypt then decrypt returns original plaintext.
+- Decryption with a different key or tenant AAD throws.
+- Bit-flip in ciphertext causes decryption to throw.
+- No plaintext or key material in any log output.
+
+## 2. Encryption key management, versioning and rotation
+
+### Existing implementation and file paths
+- **None.** No key table, no key versioning, no rotation logic. `JWT_SECRET` handling exists (weak-secret detection on feature branch) but that is for JWT signing, not credential encryption.
+
+### Missing functionality
+- No stored encryption keys, no key versions, no rotation, no re-encryption path.
+
+### Proposed changes
+- New table `encryption_keys` (section 3 migrations) with columns: `id`, `tenant_id`, `key_version`, `key_material` (encrypted at rest with a master key), `created_at`, `rotated_at`, `active` boolean.
+- Master key source (priority order): `ENCRYPTION_MASTER_KEY` env var (base64-encoded 32 bytes), then a key file path (`ENCRYPTION_MASTER_KEY_FILE`), then fail-closed error at startup if neither is set in production. For small/free deployments, a dev default may be allowed only when `NODE_ENV !== "production"` with a startup warning (mirroring existing JWT_SECRET weak-secret behavior).
+- Key versioning: each `tenant_credentials` row stores `key_version`; encryption uses the active key for that tenant; decryption looks up the key by version.
+- Rotation: add a new key version, mark previous as inactive (keep for decryption), re-encrypt credentials on a schedule or lazily on next use.
+- Key derivation: derive per-tenant encryption keys from the master key using HKDF (`crypto.hkdf`) with tenant_id as salt/info, so a compromise of one tenant key does not reveal others.
+
+### Database migrations
+- `encryption_keys` table: `id SERIAL PK`, `tenant_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES tenants(id)`, `key_version INTEGER NOT NULL`, `key_material TEXT NOT NULL` (encrypted with master key), `created_at`, `rotated_at`, `active BOOLEAN DEFAULT true`, unique constraint `(tenant_id, key_version)`, index on `(tenant_id, active)`.
+
+### API changes
+- Internal only; exposed through credential API (section 3) and a future admin rotation endpoint (out of scope for MVP if resource-constrained).
+
+### Security considerations
+- Master key never stored in DB; only derived tenant keys stored (encrypted).
+- `key_material` column encrypted at rest with the master key (defense in depth).
+- Rotation must not delete old key versions until all credentials using them are re-encrypted.
+- Fail-closed: if no master key configured in production, refuse to start (do not fall back to plaintext or a predictable key).
+
+### Tests required
+- Unit tests: HKDF derivation determinism, distinct keys per tenant, key version lookup, inactive key cannot encrypt but can decrypt, rotation preserves decryptability of old ciphertexts.
+- Startup test: missing master key in production → process refuses to start (non-zero exit).
+
+### Dependencies
+- Node.js built-in `crypto` only.
+
+### Implementation order
+- After section 1, before section 3.
+
+### Acceptance criteria
+- Each tenant gets a distinct derived key.
+- `key_version` increments on rotation; old ciphertexts still decrypt.
+- No plaintext key material in logs/DB.
+
+## 3. Tenant-scoped credential storage and API
+
+### Existing implementation and file paths
+- **None.** No `tenant_credentials` table, no credential API endpoints on `main` or feature branch.
+
+### Missing functionality
+- No place to store provider credentials (OAuth tokens, API keys, webhook secrets) scoped to a tenant.
+- No API to create/read/update/delete credentials.
+- No credential validation at ingestion time.
+
+### Proposed changes
+- New table `tenant_credentials`:
+  - `id SERIAL PK`, `tenant_id INTEGER NOT NULL REFERENCES tenants(id)`, `provider TEXT NOT NULL`, `credential_type TEXT NOT NULL`, `ciphertext TEXT NOT NULL`, `iv TEXT NOT NULL`, `auth_tag TEXT NOT NULL`, `key_version INTEGER NOT NULL`, `credential_name TEXT`, `created_at`, `updated_at`, `expires_at` (nullable).
+  - Unique constraint `(tenant_id, provider, credential_type)`; index on `(tenant_id, provider)`.
+- API (prefix `/api/credentials`, all behind `requireAuth` + tenant scoping):
+  - `POST /api/credentials` — create (validate, encrypt, store).
+  - `GET /api/credentials` — list metadata only (names, providers, types, expiry) — never plaintext or ciphertext.
+  - `GET /api/credentials/:id` — metadata only.
+  - `PATCH /api/credentials/:id` — update name/expiry; optional rotate value (re-encrypt).
+  - `DELETE /api/credentials/:id` — destroy.
+  - `GET /api/credentials/:id/validate` — provider-specific validation (section 4) without returning the secret.
+- All queries filtered by `req.user.tenant_id` (consistent with existing `businesses`/`send_log`/`config`/`pipeline_history` scoping).
+- Credential values are decrypted only inside server memory at moment of use, never returned to client, never logged.
+
+### Database migrations
+- Create `tenant_credentials` table; add indexes; no backfill.
+
+### API changes
+- New endpoints under `/api/credentials/*` — additive, non-breaking.
+- Update `swagger.js` documentation for new endpoints.
+
+### Security considerations
+- `GET` endpoints return metadata only; never ciphertext or plaintext.
+- Enforce tenant scoping on every query (defense in depth).
+- Rate-limit credential endpoints (reuse existing `express-rate-limit`).
+- Validate provider names against an allow-list.
+- Secret sanitization: ensure `logger` never receives credential values (see section 7).
+
+### Tests required
+- Integration tests: create credential, list returns metadata only (no plaintext), cross-tenant access rejected (404/403), delete removes row, update changes metadata only.
+- Unit tests: encryption integration with storage round-trip.
+- Test that listing credentials does not leak ciphertext.
+
+### Dependencies
+- None new (uses existing `pg`, `crypto`, `express`, `express-rate-limit`).
+
+### Implementation order
+- After sections 1–2.
+
+### Acceptance criteria
+- Credential plaintext never stored, never returned, never logged.
+- Cross-tenant credential access rejected.
+- Metadata-only listing works.
+
+## 4. Provider credential validation
+
+### Existing implementation and file paths
+- **None.** No provider credential validation on either branch. Connectors exist (`server/connectors/*.js`) but no validation of their credentials at rest.
+
+### Missing functionality
+- No way to verify a stored provider credential actually works before runtime use.
+- No per-provider validation logic.
+
+### Proposed changes
+- Add a validation registry keyed by provider name. Each provider defines a `validateCredential(credentialValue, tenantId)` function that performs a lightweight, read-only check against the provider (e.g. a `GET` to a provider health/metadata endpoint with the API key, using the existing `safeFetch`/SSRF-protected fetch).
+- Validation must be read-only (no writes, no charges where possible), time-boxed, and use the SSRF-protected fetcher.
+- Endpoint: `GET/POST /api/credentials/:id/validate` — returns `{ valid: boolean, checked_at, error?: string }` without exposing the secret.
+- Record last validation result in `tenant_credentials` (`last_validated_at`, `last_validation_status`, `last_validation_error` metadata only — no secret).
+
+### Database migrations
+- Add columns to `tenant_credentials`: `last_validated_at TEXT`, `last_validation_status TEXT`, `last_validation_error TEXT` (error message may be non-secret; ensure it does not echo the credential).
+
+### API changes
+- `GET/POST /api/credentials/:id/validate` — additive.
+
+### Security considerations
+- Validation calls go through `safeFetch` (SSRF protection already on feature branch) — do not bypass it for providers.
+- Validation error messages must not contain the credential or fragments of it.
+- Validation failures must not reveal whether a credential exists for a tenant the caller does not belong to (consistent 404 for cross-tenant).
+- Time-box validation (e.g. 10s) to avoid hanging.
+
+### Tests required
+- Unit tests for each provider's validation function (mock fetch): valid credential → valid, invalid → invalid with sanitized error, network error → handled gracefully.
+- Integration test: validation endpoint returns status without exposing secret; cross-tenant validation rejected.
+
+### Dependencies
+- None new (uses existing `safeFetch`).
+
+### Implementation order
+- After section 3.
+
+### Acceptance criteria
+- Valid provider credential validates successfully.
+- Invalid credential rejected without secret exposure.
+- SSRF-protected fetch used for all provider calls.
