+## 5. Security Risks
+
+1. **No tenant isolation on `main`** — All businesses, users, config, send logs, and pipeline history share one namespace. Any authenticated user can read/modify/delete another user's data.
+2. **No credential encryption on either branch** — If credentials/tokens are ever stored, they would be plaintext. No encryption library is even a dependency.
+3. **Weak/default JWT secret** — `main` CI uses `lp-ci-secret`; development default is a weak secret. Production enforcement exists only on feature branch.
+4. **No SSRF protection on `main`** — `main` `fetchWithTimeout` fetches arbitrary URLs without private-IP/metadata validation. Feature branch adds `isPrivateIp`/`validateSafeUrl`/`safeFetch`.
+5. **Incomplete RBAC** — `role` column and `requireRole` exist on feature but there is no invitation/role-assignment flow, so roles cannot practically be managed beyond registration-time default.
+6. **CI does not isolate test data** — CI reuses `lead_pipeline` database and `lp-ci-secret`; tests run against a shared dev-style configuration with no fail-closed guard.
+7. **Startup guard is environment-variable based, not module-based** — Both main and feature rely on `NODE_ENV !== "test"`; neither uses the more robust `isMainModule` (file-URL-to-path) comparison that works correctly on Windows.
+
+## 6. Recommended Recovery Strategy
+
+**Do not reimplement Phase A.5 from scratch.** The multi-tenant foundation already exists on `origin/feature/global-lead-saas` (commit `aa1f0e1` and descendants).
+
+1. **Base future Phase A.5 work on `origin/feature/global-lead-saas`, not on `main`.**  
+   `main` (`bcd5806`) is an ancestor of `feature/global-lead-saas`, so feature already contains everything in main plus the multi-tenant foundation and connector work. Branching from `main` would discard the multi-tenant foundation and force reimplementation.
+
+2. **Port the uncommitted Windows `isMainModule` fix from `cline/q2jjjkyh` onto the feature branch.**  
+   This is the only place the Windows-compatible main-module detection exists. It is a 4-line change in `server.js` (replace the `NODE_ENV !== "test"` guard with `isMainModule` using `fileURLToPath(import.meta.url) === path.resolve(process.argv[1])`). Both imports (`fileURLToPath`, `path`) already exist in `server.js`.
+
+3. **Implement the missing Phase A.5 components on top of the feature branch:**
+   - Credential encryption + key rotation (add an encryption dependency, a `tenant_credentials` table, encrypt/decrypt helpers, key-rotation endpoint)
+   - Tenant invitations + role-management API (invitation tokens, accept-invitation, add/remove members, change role)
+   - Dedicated audit-log table (auth events, admin actions) distinct from `pipeline_history`
+   - Test-only JWT/encryption secrets and fail-closed test database configuration
+   - CI enhancements (isolated test database, test-specific secrets)
+
+4. **Verify against `tests/tenant.test.js`** (already on feature branch) and add credential-security tests before considering Phase A.5 complete.
+
+
+## 7. Safest Way to Preserve Existing Connector Work
+
+The connector framework (`server/connectors/{brreg,companiesHouse,csvImport,framework,osm,webSearch}.js`) and its tests (`tests/connector*.js`, `tests/dedup.test.js`, `tests/e2a-safety.test.js`, `tests/production.test.js`) exist **only on `origin/feature/global-lead-saas`**, not on `main`.
+
+Because `main` (`bcd5806`) is an ancestor of `feature/global-lead-saas` (merge-base = `bcd5806`), merging `main` **into** `feature/global-lead-saas` would be a fast-forward / no-op: `main` contributes nothing new to feature, so **no conflicts are expected** in `server.js`, tests, migrations (none exist), `package.json`, or CI configuration, and **all connector work is automatically preserved**.
+
+**Recommended safe path:**
+1. Do **not** re-implement connectors on `main`.
+2. Create the Phase A.5 completion branch from `origin/feature/global-lead-saas` (e.g. `git checkout -b phase-a5-complete origin/feature/global-lead-saas`).
+3. Port the `isMainModule` fix from `cline/q2jjjkyh` onto that branch.
+4. Implement the missing components (encryption, invitations, credentials, audit log, test secrets, fail-closed DB, CI hardening).
+5. Run `node --check server.js`, `npm run lint`, `npm run build`, and `tests/tenant.test.js` + new credential-security tests before any merge.
+
+This preserves 100% of existing connector work while completing Phase A.5 on the branch that already contains the multi-tenant foundation.
